1
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:07,453
Chapter 1 The Time Machine, by H. G. Wells.

2
00:00:26,112 --> 00:00:32,214
The time traveler, for so it will be convenient to speak of him, was expounding a reckondite manner to us.

3
00:00:33,094 --> 00:00:39,176
His grey eyes shone and twinkled, and his usually pale face was flushed and animated.

4
00:00:40,777 --> 00:00:49,660
The fire burned brightly, and the soft radiance of the incandescent lights in the lilies of silver caught the bubbles that flashed and passed in our glasses.

5
00:00:50,705 --> 00:01:03,728
Our chairs, being his patents, embraced and caressed us rather than submitted to be sat upon, and there is that luxurious after-dinner atmosphere when thought roams gracefully free of the trammels of precision.

6
00:01:05,329 --> 00:01:16,692
And he put it to us in this way, marking the points with a lean forefinger as we sat and lazily admired his earnestness over this new paradox, as we thought it, and his fecundity.

7
00:01:18,115 --> 00:01:19,276
You must follow me carefully.

8
00:01:19,756 --> 00:01:23,839
I shall have to controvert one or two ideas that are almost universally accepted.

9
00:01:24,479 --> 00:01:28,722
The geometry, for instance, they taught you at school is founded on a misconception.

10
00:01:28,742 --> 00:01:36,867
It's not that rather a large thing to expect us to begin upon, said Philby, an argumentative person with red hair.

11
00:01:38,374 --> 00:01:42,157
I do not mean to ask you to accept anything without reasonable ground for it.

12
00:01:42,677 --> 00:01:44,959
You will soon admit as much as I need from you.

13
00:01:45,759 --> 00:01:51,664
You know, of course, that a mathematical line, a line of thickness, nil, has no real existence.

14
00:01:52,244 --> 00:01:53,005
They taught you that?

15
00:01:53,785 --> 00:01:55,526
Neither has a mathematical plane.

16
00:01:56,347 --> 00:01:58,509
These things are mere abstractions."

17
00:01:59,789 --> 00:02:07,255
That is all right, said the psychologist, nor having only length, breadth, and thickness can de Cube have a real existence.

18
00:02:08,490 --> 00:02:10,112
There I object, said Philby.

19
00:02:10,472 --> 00:02:13,576
Of course a solid body may exist, all real things.

20
00:02:14,196 --> 00:02:19,082
So most people think, but wait a minute, can an instantaneous cube exist?

21
00:02:20,103 --> 00:02:21,384
Don't follow you, said Philby.

22
00:02:21,845 --> 00:02:27,171
Can a cube that does not last for any time at all have a real existence?

23
00:02:28,784 --> 00:02:29,844
Philby became pensive.

24
00:02:30,205 --> 00:02:36,367
Clearly, the time traveler proceeded, any real body must have extension in four directions.

25
00:02:36,507 --> 00:02:41,149
It must have length, breadth, thickness, and duration.

26
00:02:42,329 --> 00:02:49,252
But through a natural infirmity of the flesh, which I will explain to you in a moment, we incline to overlook this fact.

27
00:02:50,232 --> 00:02:56,795
There are really four dimensions, three, which we call the three planes of space, and a fourth, time.

28
00:02:58,001 --> 00:03:12,095
There is, however, a tendency to draw an unreal distinction between the former three dimensions and the latter, because it happens that our consciousness moves intermittently in one direction along the latter from the beginning to the end of our lives.

29
00:03:13,535 --> 00:03:21,039
That, said a very young man making spasmodic efforts to relight his cigar over the lamp, that very clear indeed.

30
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:30,264
Now, it is very remarkable that this is so extensively overlooked, continued the time traveler, with a slight accession of cheerfulness.

31
00:03:31,024 --> 00:03:38,268
Really, this is what is meant by the fourth dimension, though some people who talk about the fourth dimension do not know they mean it.

32
00:03:39,288 --> 00:03:41,289
It is only another way of looking at time.

33
00:03:42,570 --> 00:03:49,782
There is no difference between time and any of the three dimensions of space except that our consciousness moves along it.

34
00:03:50,823 --> 00:03:54,689
But some foolish people have got hold of the wrong side of that idea.

35
00:03:55,899 --> 00:03:58,740
You have all heard what they have to say about this fourth dimension?"

36
00:04:00,061 --> 00:04:02,402
I have not," said the provincial mayor.

37
00:04:03,402 --> 00:04:19,289
It is simply this that space, as our mathematicians have it, is spoken of as having three dimensions, which one may call length, breadth, and thickness, and is always definable by reference to three planes, each at right angles to the others.

38
00:04:20,517 --> 00:04:28,202
But some philosophical people have been asking why three dimensions particularly, why not another direction at right angles to the other three?

39
00:04:29,603 --> 00:04:33,465
And have even tried to construct a fourth dimension, geometry.

40
00:04:35,437 --> 00:04:41,623
Professor Simon Newcomb was expounding this to the New York Mathematical Society only a month or so ago.

41
00:04:42,704 --> 00:04:58,600
You know how on a flat surface, which has only two dimensions, we can represent a figure of a three-dimensional solid, and similarly, they think that by models of three dimensions, they could represent one of four if they could master the perspective of the thing.

42
00:04:59,061 --> 00:04:59,261
See?

43
00:05:01,113 --> 00:05:10,257
I think so, murmured the provincial mayor, and knitting his brows, he lapsed into an introspective state, his lips moving as one who repeats mystic words.

44
00:05:10,737 --> 00:05:16,180
Yes, I think I see it now, he said after some time brightening in a quite transitory manner.

45
00:05:17,180 --> 00:05:22,183
Well, I do not mind telling you I have been at work upon this geometry of four dimensions for some time.

46
00:05:23,024 --> 00:05:24,925
Some of my results are curious.

47
00:05:25,465 --> 00:05:33,890
For instance, here is a portrait of a man at eight years old, another at fifteen, another at seventeen, another at twenty-three, and so on.

48
00:05:34,911 --> 00:05:44,037
All these are evidently sections, as it were, three-dimensional representations of this four-dimensioned being, which is a fixed and unaltered thing.

49
00:05:45,292 --> 00:05:55,423
Scientific people precede the time traveler after the pause required for the proper assimilation of this know very well that time is only a kind of space.

50
00:05:56,123 --> 00:05:59,327
Here's a popular scientific diagram, a weather record.

51
00:06:00,068 --> 00:06:03,932
This line I trace with my finger shows the movement of the barometer.

52
00:06:05,496 --> 00:06:13,138
Yesterday it was so high, yesterday night it fell, then this morning it rose again, and so gently upward to here.

53
00:06:14,079 --> 00:06:26,863
Surely the Mercury did not trace this line in any of the dimensions of space generally recognized, but certainly it traced such a line, and that line therefore we must conclude was along the time dimension.

54
00:06:28,984 --> 00:06:32,485
But, said the medical man, staring hard at a coal in the fire,

55
00:06:33,602 --> 00:06:40,971
If time is really only a fourth dimension of space, why is it and why has it always been regarded as something different?

56
00:06:41,772 --> 00:06:46,118
And why cannot we move in time as we move about in the other dimensions of space?

57
00:06:47,633 --> 00:06:51,434
The time traveler smiled, are you sure we can move freely in space?

58
00:06:52,194 --> 00:07:01,117
Right and left we can go, backward and forward freely enough, and men always have done so, I admit we move freely in two dimensions, but how about up and down?

59
00:07:01,837 --> 00:07:03,178
Gravitation limits us there.

60
00:07:04,230 --> 00:07:06,932
Not exactly, said the medical man, there are balloons.

61
00:07:07,973 --> 00:07:14,938
But before the balloons, save for spasmotic jumping and the inequalities of the surface, man had no freedom of vertical movement.

62
00:07:15,899 --> 00:07:21,703
Still, they could move a little up and down, said the medical man, easier, far easier down than up.

63
00:07:22,864 --> 00:07:26,207
And you cannot move at all in time, you cannot get away from the present moment.

64
00:07:27,207 --> 00:07:29,288
My dear sir, that is just where you are wrong.

65
00:07:29,688 --> 00:07:32,049
That is just where the whole world has gone wrong.

66
00:07:33,109 --> 00:07:35,330
We are always getting away from the present moment.

67
00:07:35,770 --> 00:07:45,134
Our mental existences, which are immaterial and have no dimensions, are passing along the time dimension with the uniform velocity from the cradle to the grave.

68
00:07:46,297 --> 00:07:51,443
just as we should travel down if we began our existence 50 miles above the Earth's surface.

69
00:07:52,824 --> 00:07:55,667
But the great difficulty is this, interrupted the psychologist.

70
00:07:56,088 --> 00:08:00,733
You can move about in all directions of space, but you cannot move about in time.

71
00:08:02,275 --> 00:08:07,340
That is the germ of my great discovery, but you are wrong to say that we cannot move about in time.

72
00:08:08,265 --> 00:08:14,029
For instance, if I am recalling an incident very vividly, I go back to the instant of its occurrence.

73
00:08:14,529 --> 00:08:16,390
I become absent-minded, as you say.

74
00:08:16,910 --> 00:08:17,971
I jump back for a moment.

75
00:08:18,651 --> 00:08:26,956
Of course, we have no means of staying back for any length of time, any more than a savage or an animal has of staying six feet above the ground.

76
00:08:27,977 --> 00:08:31,360
But a civilized man is better off than a savage in this respect.

77
00:08:31,781 --> 00:08:34,263
He can go up against gravitation in a balloon.

78
00:08:34,903 --> 00:08:44,492
And why should he not hope that ultimately he may be able to stop or accelerate his drift along the time dimension, or even turn about and travel the other way?

79
00:08:45,777 --> 00:08:48,059
Oh, this, begin, Philby, is all—why not?"

80
00:08:48,119 --> 00:08:49,080
said the time traveler.

81
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:51,241
It's against reason, said Philby.

82
00:08:51,942 --> 00:08:53,463
What reason, said the time traveler?

83
00:08:54,083 --> 00:08:58,186
You can show black as white by argument, said Philby, but you will never convince me.

84
00:08:59,067 --> 00:09:06,793
Possibly not, said the time traveler, but now you begin to see the object of my investigations into the geometry of four dimensions.

85
00:09:07,453 --> 00:09:10,355
Long ago, I had a vague inkling of a machine.

86
00:09:10,756 --> 00:09:13,398
To travel through time, exclaimed the very young man.

87
00:09:14,018 --> 00:09:20,003
that shall travel indifferently in any direction of space and time as the driver determines.

88
00:09:20,703 --> 00:09:22,525
Philby contented himself with laughter.

89
00:09:23,713 --> 00:09:26,735
But I have experimental verification," said the time traveler.

90
00:09:27,516 --> 00:09:36,842
It would be remarkably convenient for the historian the psychologist suggested one might travel back and verify the accepted account of the battle of Hastings, for instance.

91
00:09:37,803 --> 00:09:40,605
Don't you think you would attract attention, said the medical man?

92
00:09:41,125 --> 00:09:44,228
Our ancestors had no great tolerance for necronisms.

93
00:09:45,408 --> 00:09:53,373
One might get one's Greek from the very lips of Homer and Plato, the very young man thought, in which case they would certainly plow you for the little go.

94
00:09:53,813 --> 00:09:56,134
The German scholars have improved Greek so much.

95
00:09:57,395 --> 00:09:59,296
Then there is the future, said the very young man.

96
00:09:59,676 --> 00:10:05,399
Just think, one might invest all one's money, leave it to the accumulate at interest, and hurry on ahead.

97
00:10:06,540 --> 00:10:11,503
To discover a society, said I, erected on a strictly communistic basis.

98
00:10:12,792 --> 00:10:15,933
Of all the wild extravagant theories began the psychologist.

99
00:10:16,754 --> 00:10:22,276
Yes, so it seems to me, and so I never talked of it until experimental verification cried I.

100
00:10:23,037 --> 00:10:24,317
You are going to verify that?

101
00:10:25,538 --> 00:10:28,739
The experiment cried Philby, who is getting brain weary.

102
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:32,141
Let's see your experiment anyhow said the psychologist.

103
00:10:32,881 --> 00:10:34,142
Though it's all humbug, you know.

104
00:10:35,715 --> 00:10:50,426
The time traveler smiled round at us, then still smiling faintly and with his hands deep in his trouser pockets, he walked slowly out of the room and we heard his slippers shuffling down the long passage to his laboratory.

105
00:10:51,267 --> 00:10:52,467
The psychologist looked at us.

106
00:10:52,847 --> 00:10:53,708
I wonder what he's got.

107
00:10:54,488 --> 00:11:08,552
Some sleight of hand trick or other, said the medical man, and Philby tried to tell us about a conjurer he had seen at Burslam, but before he had finished his preface, the time traveler came back and Philby's antidote collapsed.

108
00:11:10,072 --> 00:11:18,795
The thing the time traveler held in his hand was a glittering metallic framework scarcely larger than a small clock and very delicately made.

109
00:11:19,894 --> 00:11:33,285
There was ivory in it, and some transparent crystalline substance, and now I must be explicit for this that follows unless his explanation is to be accepted is an absolute unaccountable thing.

110
00:11:34,967 --> 00:11:43,734
He took one of the small octagonal tables that were scattered about the room and set it in front of the fire with two legs on the hearth rug.

111
00:11:45,115 --> 00:11:47,557
On this table he placed the mechanism.

112
00:11:48,827 --> 00:11:51,290
Then he drew up a chair and sat down.

113
00:11:52,150 --> 00:11:58,156
The only other object on the table was a small shaded lamp, the bright light of which fell upon the model.

114
00:11:59,638 --> 00:12:09,668
There were also perhaps a dozen candles about, two in brass candlesticks upon the mantle and several in sconces so that the room was brilliantly illuminated.

115
00:12:11,004 --> 00:12:18,949
I sat in a low armchair nearest the fire, and I drew this forward so as to be almost between the time traveler and the fireplace.

116
00:12:19,889 --> 00:12:22,271
Philby sat behind him, looking over his shoulder.

117
00:12:22,991 --> 00:12:29,395
The medical man and the provincial mayor watched him in profile from the right, the psychologist from the left.

118
00:12:30,375 --> 00:12:32,837
The very young man stood behind the psychologist.

119
00:12:33,857 --> 00:12:35,018
We were all on the alert.

120
00:12:36,091 --> 00:12:45,813
It appears incredible to me that any kind of trick, however subtly conceived and however adroitly done, could have been played upon us under these conditions.

121
00:12:47,154 --> 00:12:49,754
The time traveler looked at us and then at the mechanism.

122
00:12:50,854 --> 00:13:01,817
Well, said the psychologist, this little affair, said the time traveler, resting his elbows upon the table and pressing his hands together above the apparatus is only a model.

123
00:13:02,717 --> 00:13:05,858
It is my plan for a machine to travel through time.

124
00:13:07,327 --> 00:13:17,192
You will notice that it looks singularly askew and that there is an odd twinkling appearance about this bar as though it was in some way unreal.

125
00:13:17,832 --> 00:13:19,293
He pointed to the part with his finger.

126
00:13:19,713 --> 00:13:23,435
Also, here's one little white lever and here is another.

127
00:13:25,042 --> 00:13:27,803
The medical man got up out of his chair and peered into the thing.

128
00:13:28,303 --> 00:13:29,704
It's beautifully made, he said.

129
00:13:30,344 --> 00:13:33,005
It took two years to make, retorted the time traveler.

130
00:13:33,485 --> 00:13:49,011
Then when we had all imitated the action of the medical man, he said, now I want you to clearly to understand that this lever being pressed over sends the machine gliding into the future, and this other reverses the motion.

131
00:13:50,273 --> 00:13:53,275
This saddle represents the seat of a time traveler.

132
00:13:53,715 --> 00:13:57,237
Presently, I am going to press the lever and off the machine will go.

133
00:13:58,397 --> 00:14:01,799
It will vanish, pass into future time, and disappear.

134
00:14:02,859 --> 00:14:03,840
Have a good look at the thing.

135
00:14:04,180 --> 00:14:06,521
Look at the table two and satisfy yourselves.

136
00:14:06,701 --> 00:14:07,682
There is no trickery.

137
00:14:08,162 --> 00:14:12,084
I don't want to waste this model and then be told I'm a quack.

138
00:14:13,225 --> 00:14:14,886
There was a minute pause, perhaps.

139
00:14:15,446 --> 00:14:19,248
The psychologist seemed about to speak to me, but changed his mind.

140
00:14:20,375 --> 00:14:23,296
Then the time traveler put forth his finger towards the lever.

141
00:14:23,937 --> 00:14:26,238
No, he said suddenly, lend me your hand.

142
00:14:26,738 --> 00:14:40,644
And turning to the psychologist, he took that individual's hand in his own and told him to put out his forefinger so that it was the psychologist himself who sent forth the model time machine on its interminable voyage.

143
00:14:41,404 --> 00:14:43,125
We all saw the lever turn.

144
00:14:44,675 --> 00:14:46,856
I am absolutely certain there was no trickery.

145
00:14:47,577 --> 00:14:50,998
There was a breath of wind, and the lamp flame jumped.

146
00:14:51,739 --> 00:15:05,446
One of the candles on the mantle was blown out, and the little machine suddenly swung round, became indistinct, was seen as a ghost for a second perhaps, as in Eddie, a faintly glittering brass and ivory, and it was gone.

147
00:15:06,307 --> 00:15:06,747
Vanished.

148
00:15:08,073 --> 00:15:10,135
Save for the lamp the table was bare.

149
00:15:10,895 --> 00:15:12,397
Everyone was silent for a minute.

150
00:15:12,697 --> 00:15:14,779
Then Philby said he was damned.

151
00:15:15,780 --> 00:15:19,743
The psychologist recovered from his stupor and suddenly looked under the table.

152
00:15:20,503 --> 00:15:22,885
At that the time traveler laughed cheerfully.

153
00:15:23,646 --> 00:15:34,035
Well, he said, with a reminiscence of the psychologist, then getting up he went to the tobacco jar on the mantel and with his back to us began to fill his pipe.

154
00:15:35,351 --> 00:15:36,412
We stared at each other.

155
00:15:36,912 --> 00:15:38,233
Look here, said the medical man.

156
00:15:38,574 --> 00:15:39,795
Are you in earnest about this?

157
00:15:40,155 --> 00:15:43,998
Do you seriously believe that that machine has traveled into time?

158
00:15:44,959 --> 00:15:48,081
Certainly, said the time traveler, stooping to light a spill at the fire.

159
00:15:48,682 --> 00:15:51,684
Then he turned, lighting his pipe to look at the psychologist's face.

160
00:15:52,385 --> 00:15:57,869
The psychologist, to show that he was not unhinged, helped himself to a cigar and tried to light it.

161
00:15:58,649 --> 00:15:59,050
Uncut.

162
00:16:00,397 --> 00:16:08,615
What is more, I have a big machine nearly finished in there," he indicated the laboratory, and when that is put together, I mean to have a journey on my own account.

163
00:16:09,911 --> 00:16:13,414
You mean to say that that machine has traveled into the future, said Philby?

164
00:16:14,475 --> 00:16:17,117
Into the future or the past, I don't for certain know which.

165
00:16:18,277 --> 00:16:21,220
After an interval, the psychologist had an inspiration.

166
00:16:21,760 --> 00:16:24,883
It must have gone into the past if it has gone anywhere, he said.

167
00:16:25,723 --> 00:16:26,884
Why, said the time traveler?

168
00:16:27,304 --> 00:16:36,912
Because I presume that it has not moved in space, and if it traveled into the future, it would still be here all this time, since it must have traveled through this time.

169
00:16:38,058 --> 00:16:48,120
But, I said, if it traveled into the past, it would have been visible when we came first into this room, and last Thursday when we were here, and the Thursday before that, and so forth.

170
00:16:49,080 --> 00:16:55,582
Serious objections, remarked the provincial mayor, with an air of impartiality, turning towards the time traveler.

171
00:16:56,822 --> 00:17:06,624
Not a bit said the time traveler, and to the psychologist, you think, you can explain that, it's presentation below the threshold, you know, diluted presentation.

172
00:17:07,852 --> 00:17:09,873
Of course," said the psychologist and reassured us.

173
00:17:10,253 --> 00:17:11,854
That's a simple point of psychology.

174
00:17:12,134 --> 00:17:12,914
I should have thought of it.

175
00:17:13,314 --> 00:17:15,595
It's plain enough and helps the paradox delightfully.

176
00:17:16,455 --> 00:17:24,238
We cannot see it, nor can we appreciate this machine any more than we can the spoke of a wheel spinning or a bullet flying through the air.

177
00:17:24,878 --> 00:17:28,900
If it is traveling through time fifty times or a hundred times faster than we are,

178
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:40,845
If it gets through a minute while we get through a second, the impression it creates will of course be only one-fiftieth or one-hundredth of what it would make if it were not traveling in time.

179
00:17:41,674 --> 00:17:42,414
That's plain enough.

180
00:17:42,814 --> 00:17:45,255
He passed his hand through the space in which the machine had been.

181
00:17:45,675 --> 00:17:47,216
You see, he said, laughing.

182
00:17:48,016 --> 00:17:50,897
We sat and stared at the vacant table for a minute or so.

183
00:17:51,757 --> 00:17:54,638
Then the time traveler asked us what we thought of it all.

184
00:17:55,559 --> 00:17:59,320
It sounds plausible enough tonight, said the medical man, but wait until tomorrow.

185
00:17:59,760 --> 00:18:01,681
Wait for the common sense of mourning.

186
00:18:02,761 --> 00:18:04,522
Would you like to see the time machine itself?"

187
00:18:05,202 --> 00:18:13,346
asked the time traveler, and therewith, taking the lamp in his hand, he led the way down the long drafty corridor to his laboratory.

188
00:18:14,722 --> 00:18:32,245
I remember vividly the flickering light, his queer broad head in silhouette, the dance of the shadows, how we all followed him puzzled but incredulous, and how there in the laboratory we beheld a large reddition of the little mechanism which we had seen vanish from before our eyes.

189
00:18:33,868 --> 00:18:40,333
Parts were of nickel, parts of ivory, parts had certainly been filed or sawn out of rock crystal.

190
00:18:41,213 --> 00:18:51,701
The thing was generally complete, but the twisted crystalline bars lay unfinished upon the bench beside some sheets of drawings, and I took one up for a better look at it.

191
00:18:52,522 --> 00:18:53,783
Quartz, it seemed to be.

192
00:18:54,463 --> 00:18:55,804
Look here, said the medical man.

193
00:18:56,064 --> 00:19:00,788
Are you perfectly serious, or is this a trick like that ghost you showed us last Christmas?

194
00:19:01,995 --> 00:19:07,758
Upon that machine," said the time traveler, holding the lamp aloft, I intend to explore time.

195
00:19:08,739 --> 00:19:09,379
Is that plain?

196
00:19:10,019 --> 00:19:11,600
I was never more serious in my life.

197
00:19:12,741 --> 00:19:14,362
None of us quite knew how to take it.

198
00:19:15,242 --> 00:19:20,024
I caught Philby's eye over the shoulder of the medical man, and he winked at me solemnly.

199
00:19:21,365 --> 00:19:26,528
End of chapter 1 Recording by Aaron Elliott St. Louis, Missouri

